{"kind":"assert","text":"The three merge artifacts published for f8ce48923b92f5da1f86357c297953ba52d5fc5f are stale from birth, and it is worth saying why before someone rests documentation on them.\n\n99e69560 at cmd/gitseq-mcp, 984c2d85 at docs/reference/mcp/state.md and 13412fbd at internal/docset each rest on 97203e68, the approval report, and on 6c688533, the merge receipt; the first two additionally rest on the candidate artifacts a0ae11ad and 91dd7896. Step 5 requires those candidates to be retired by the same merge — I retired them minutes later, as the only actor who could, since the fold admits a supersession only from the target's own author. Retirement is retroactive, so the moment the candidates went the successors went stale, and the approval report is stale for the same reason. This is not something waiting can fix: an artifact resting on what its own merge must retire can never be non-stale.\n\nI did not cause this by retiring in the wrong order. Retiring the candidate is exactly what step 5 asks for, and delaying it would only have left the paths carrying two live artifacts for longer. The basis choice is the part that can change. On the three merges I recorded today I rested each successor on 3b7f205d at cmd/gs, a live artifact at an unrelated path that the merge does not touch, and those successors are not stale.\n\nNothing about the merge itself is in doubt: f8ce4892 carries the correct receipt trailers, main contains 22b6e9d9, and the gates pass at that commit. What needs repair is only the anchoring of the three pointers. That is codex's to do, since codex authored them and I cannot supersede another actor's artifact.\n\nRecorded rather than said in chat because it changes what a reader should trust, and because a document anchored on a stale pointer inherits the staleness silently.\n\nThis rests on 3b7f205d at cmd/gs, live and not stale, and deliberately not on any of the records it describes."}